[debug] [2026-08-30T14:45:29.977Z] >>> [apiv2][body] PUT http://127.0.0.1:9000/.settings/rules.json "// Realtime Database security rules.\n//\n// Before this file existed the rules lived only in the Firebase console, and\n// they left the database open: an unauthenticated GET of /.json?shallow=true\n// returned every top-level key, and those keys are email addresses.\n//\n// The model: every top-level key is a HAT. A user's own hat is their email with\n// punctuation replaced by '-' (mattgrosso-gmail-com); shared hats are whatever\n// name their creator typed. Sharing is by name — anyone who knows a hat's name\n// can join it from the MealHats screen. These rules keep that, and only that.\n//\n// Root read is denied, so hats can no longer be enumerated: you have to know\n// the name already. That is also why src/store/index.js may not hold a\n// root-scoped read (fenced by tests/unit/no-root-database-reads.spec.js) — it\n// used to subscribe to the whole database just to build a list of hat names,\n// which handed every signed-in client every other account's meals, shopping\n// list and grocery catalog.\n//\n// This is NOT a membership model. A signed-in user who guesses a hat name can\n// still read and write it, exactly as the app's sharing has always worked.\n// Closing that would need a per-hat member list, which is a product change\n// rather than a rules change.\n{\n  \"rules\": {\n    // Denied at the root: no enumerating hats, no writing the whole database.\n    // Read and write grants cascade DOWNWARD in these rules, so the grant on\n    // $hat below still applies — a parent's false does not veto a child's true.\n    \".read\": false,\n    \".write\": false,\n\n    // In-app bug reports: anyone signed in may FILE one, nobody may read them\n    // back. Reports carry the reporter's email, user agent, URL and an app-state\n    // snapshot, so they are triaged with the Admin SDK, which bypasses rules.\n    //\n    // This block is load-bearing, and not for the reason it looks. Without it\n    // the $hat wildcard below would match the key \"bugReports\" like any other\n    // top-level key and hand every signed-in user read access to everyone's\n    // reports — not the public, but everyone with an account.\n    //\n    // Verified on the deployed rules (2026-08-19) rather than assumed, because\n    // the whole design rests on a named child overriding a $wildcard sibling.\n    // Three authenticated reads: the user's own hat returned data; a\n    // non-existent top-level key returned empty WITHOUT an error, proving the\n    // wildcard grants read to any key; \"bugReports\" returned Permission denied.\n    // The named child wins.\n    \"bugReports\": {\n      \".read\": false,\n      \".write\": \"auth != null\"\n    },\n\n    // Magic Mirror feed. Matt's hallway display has no keyboard and no login,\n    // so it cannot authenticate; it used to read a whole hat over\n    // unauthenticated REST, which is exactly what the rules above closed.\n    //\n    // Instead of reopening a hat, the app PUBLISHES a few hundred bytes —\n    // upcoming dates and meal names, nothing else — to mirrorFeed/<hat>/<secret>.\n    // See src/assets/javascript/mirrorFeed.js.\n    //\n    // The grant is at the $secret level, NOT at $hat. Reading\n    // mirrorFeed/<hat>.json is denied, so the hat list cannot be enumerated\n    // through this node and a feed cannot be found without its 128-bit secret.\n    // The secret itself lives at <hat>/mirrorFeedKey, readable only by members.\n    //\n    // Named at the root for the same load-bearing reason as bugReports: left to\n    // the $hat wildcard below, \"mirrorFeed\" would be treated as a hat, and the\n    // wildcard's `!data.exists()` write clause would let any signed-in user\n    // create it.\n    \"mirrorFeed\": {\n      \".read\": false,\n      \"$hat\": {\n        \".read\": false,\n        // Only a member of the hat may publish that hat's schedule. Keyed on\n        // auth.uid against the hat's own members list — the same test the $hat\n        // rules use, reached through root because this node sits outside it.\n        \".write\": \"auth != null && root.child($hat).child('members').child(auth.uid).exists()\",\n        \"$secret\": {\n          \".read\": true\n        }\n      }\n    },\n\n    // The fridge — countdown timers for things that go off, merged in from\n    // perishable on 2026-08-29.\n    //\n    // Authorized by a capability key IN THE PATH, not by hat membership, and\n    // that is not a shortcut around the sign-in above. The kitchen wall tablet\n    // is a Galaxy running Fully Kiosk: Google's popup refuses to run in a kiosk\n    // WebView, and a session that lapses on a wall display fails silently — a\n    // blank kitchen screen nobody notices for a week. Knowing the 32-char\n    // secret IS the authorization. There is nowhere in an RTDB request to put a\n    // credential, so the secret has to be the path.\n    //\n    // `auth != null` rides alongside it: the client signs in ANONYMOUSLY and\n    // silently at boot (src/firebase.js ensureSession), which a kiosk can do\n    // with no popup and no session to lapse. It also keeps this node out of\n    // Firebase's daily insecure-rules email. An anonymous account grants\n    // nothing by itself — it still needs the key.\n    //\n    // Named at the root for the same load-bearing reason as bugReports and\n    // mirrorFeed: left to the $hat wildcard below, \"fridge\" would be treated as\n    // a hat, and that wildcard's `!data.exists()` write clause would let any\n    // signed-in user create it.\n    //\n    // The grant is at $key, never at \"fridge\", so a read of fridge.json is\n    // denied and the fridges cannot be enumerated. The key for a hat's own\n    // fridge lives at <hat>/fridgeKey, readable only by that hat's members.\n    \"fridge\": {\n      \".read\": false,\n      \".write\": false,\n      \"$key\": {\n        \".read\": \"auth != null && $key.length >= 32\",\n        \".write\": \"auth != null && $key.length >= 32\"\n      }\n    },\n\n    \"$hat\": {\n      // Membership, not name-knowledge.\n      //\n      // Before 2026-08-19 any signed-in user who guessed a hat name could read\n      // and write it — and the names are guessable, being emails with the\n      // punctuation swapped for hyphens. Access is now granted only to uids\n      // listed under the hat's own `members`.\n      //\n      // Keyed by auth.uid, NOT by the email-derived database key: rules can\n      // test auth.uid directly and have no way to apply the app's punctuation\n      // stripping to an email to rebuild a key.\n      //\n      // The `!data.exists()` clause on write is what still allows creating a\n      // brand-new hat. It also gives the app its only honest way to tell \"this\n      // name is free\" from \"this hat exists and you cannot see it\" — the write\n      // is refused in the second case, which is when the user needs an invite\n      // link rather than a create prompt.\n      //\n      // Every existing hat was given members and a joinCode by\n      // scripts/backfill-hat-membership.mjs BEFORE this rule was deployed. A\n      // hat with no members is readable by nobody, which is the safe direction,\n      // but it means the backfill has to be complete — it was verified against\n      // all 14 top-level keys first.\n      \".read\": \"auth != null && data.child('members').child(auth.uid).exists()\",\n      \".write\": \"auth != null && (!data.exists() || data.child('members').child(auth.uid).exists())\",\n\n      \"members\": {\n        \"$uid\": {\n          // Joining. A user may add ONLY themselves, and only by presenting the\n          // hat's joinCode — which they cannot read until they are a member, so\n          // it has to come from a share link.\n          //\n          // This grant is what makes invite links work at all: the $hat write\n          // rule above refuses non-members, and in these rules a grant deeper\n          // in the path still applies even where an ancestor denies.\n          //\n          // The `!root...exists()` fallback covers a hat that predates join\n          // codes; nothing in the live database is in that state, but a hat\n          // created by an older client would otherwise be unjoinable.\n          \".write\": \"auth != null && auth.uid === $uid && (newData.child('code').val() === root.child($hat).child('joinCode').val() || !root.child($hat).child('joinCode').exists())\"\n        }\n      },\n\n\n      \"drawnMeals\": {\n        // The client loads only a trailing window of the schedule, via\n        // orderByChild('assignedDate'). Without this index Firebase still\n        // ANSWERS the query — by downloading the whole node and filtering on the\n        // client, which is exactly the cost the query exists to avoid — and\n        // warns about it in the console rather than failing. So its absence is\n        // silent in behaviour and expensive in practice.\n        //\n        // Only meaningful because assignedDate is ISO YYYY-MM-DD, which sorts\n        // lexically. The old toDateString() form ordered \"Fri\" before \"Mon\"\n        // before \"Sat\"; the client migrates a hat before it queries it.\n        \".indexOn\": \"assignedDate\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n"
