y.length >= 32\",\n        \".write\": \"auth != null && $key.length >= 32\"\n      }\n    },\n\n    \"$hat\": {\n      // Membership, not name-knowledge.\n      //\n      // Before 2026-08-19 any signed-in user who guessed a hat name could read\n      // and write it — and the names are guessable, being emails with the\n      // punctuation swapped for hyphens. Access is now granted only to uids\n      // listed under the hat's own `members`.\n      //\n      // Keyed by auth.uid, NOT by the email-derived database key: rules can\n      // test auth.uid directly and have no way to apply the app's punctuation\n      // stripping to an email to rebuild a key.\n      //\n      // The `!data.exists()` clause on write is what still allows creating a\n      // brand-new hat. It also gives the app its only honest way to tell \"this\n      // name is free\" from \"this hat exists and you cannot see it\" — the write\n      // is refused in the second case, which is when the user needs an invite\n      // link rather than a create prompt.\n      //\n      // Every existing hat was given members and a joinCode by\n      // scripts/backfill-hat-membership.mjs BEFORE this rule was deployed. A\n      // hat with no members is readable by nobody, which is the safe direction,\n      // but it means the backfill has to be complete — it was verified against\n      // all 14 top-level keys first.\n      \".read\": \"auth != null && data.child('members').child(auth.uid).exists()\",\n      \".write\": \"auth != null && (!data.exists() || data.child('members').child(auth.uid).exists())\",\n\n      \"members\": {\n        \"$uid\": {\n          // Joining. A user may add ONLY themselves, and only by presenting the\n          // hat's joinCode — which they cannot read until they are a member, so\n          // it has to come from a share link.\n          //\n          // This grant is what makes invite links work at all: the $hat write\n          // rule above refuses non-members, and in th